25, 0.01425933837890625, -0.010009765625, -0.00846099853515625, 0.022003173828125, 0.051727294921875, 0.050262451171875, 0.03179931640625, 0.01149749755859375, 0.026763916015625, -0.0010137557983398438, -0.038665771484375, 0.002567291259765625, 0.007450103759765625, 0.0174102783203125, -0.0244598388671875, -0.00820159912109375, 0.01213836669921875, -0.00992584228515625, -0.0362548828125, 0.0036602020263671875, -0.01374053955078125, 0.0090484619140625, -0.0184173583984375, -0.027435302734375, -0.023651123046875, 0.00787353515625, -0.00530242919921875, -0.0276947021484375, 0.021575927734375, -0.0188446044921875, 0.033355712890625, 0.016754150390625, -0.047943115234375, -0.026947021484375, -0.00250244140625, 0.0077362060546875, -0.0250701904296875, 0.0122222900390625, 0.0162353515625, -0.00830841064453125, 0.0032634735107421875, -0.0247344970703125, 0.0004887580871582031, 0.04302978515625, -0.00750732421875, 0.01538848876953125, 0.00939178466796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1,2]. However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations that govern a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process fundamental to system analysis and control design. While previous works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions [3] and Clifford algebras [4], these approaches either do not fully address the multigrade structure of GA or are limited to specific algebraic settings. In particular, prior extensions often treat only scalar or vector components, or assume commutativity that does not hold in general GA settings. Thus, a comprehensive and consistent definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra remains lacking. Our work addresses this gap by providing a formal definition and analysis of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in GA, with explicit attention to the algebra\u2019s non-commutative structure.\n\nThe main contributions of this work are as follows: (1) we introduce a precise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras of signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5; (2) we establish key properties of this transform, including linearity, the shifting theorem, and the treatment of non-commutative products; (3) we clarify the relationship and improvements over previous approaches for quaternions and Clifford algebras; and (4) we demonstrate the practical benefits of our approach through a concrete example involving RLC circuit analysis.\n\nOur definition of the Laplace transform in GA is as follows: for a multivector-valued function \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q), the Laplace transform \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) \\) is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex parameter and the exponential is interpreted grade-wise: \\( f(t) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\), and the Laplace transform is applied separately to each grade component. The non-commutative nature of the geometric product is addressed by ensuring that the scalar exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) commutes with all multivector components, so the integral is well-defined. For cases where the Laplace parameter \\( s \\) is itself a multivector, care must be taken, and our definition restricts \\( s \\) to scalar or complex values to avoid ambiguities arising from non-commutativity.\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is motivated by computational tractability: as p + q increases, the dimension of the algebra grows exponentially (e.g., for p + q = 5, the algebra has 32 dimensions), making both analytical and numerical computations increasingly impractical. Most physical and engineering applications, such as the spacetime algebra (1,3) and the Euclidean algebra (3,0), fall within this range, ensuring that our results are broadly applicable while avoiding the intractability of higher-dimensional cases. For example, in robotics and electromagnetism, the use of (3,0) or (1,3) algebras suffices for most modeling needs, while larger signatures are rarely encountered in practice.\n\nOur approach generalizes previous work on quaternions [3] and Clifford algebras [4] by providing a unified treatment for all grades in GA, rather than focusing on specific subalgebras or assuming commutativity. Unlike quaternionic Laplace transforms, which are limited to four-dimensional algebras and often treat only scalar and vector parts, our definition applies to arbitrary multivector-valued functions and respects the full multigrade structure. Compared to earlier Clifford algebra approaches, we explicitly address the non-commutative geometric product by grade-wise transformation and by restricting the Laplace parameter to scalars or complex numbers, thus avoiding ambiguities.\n\nA key challenge in transforming multivector-valued functions is the non-commutative nature of the geometric product. Our definition ensures that the Laplace transform is applied linearly and grade-wise, so that the relationships between grades are preserved. However, if the Laplace parameter \\( s \\) were allowed to be a general multivector, the non-commutativity could lead to ambiguities in the definition of the exponential and the integral. By restricting \\( s \\) to scalars or complex numbers, we avoid these issues and ensure that the transform is well-defined for all multivector-valued functions of practical interest.\n\nAs with the classical Laplace transform, not all functions admit a Laplace transform in GA. For example, functions that grow faster than exponentially, such as \\( f(t) = e^{t^2} \\mathbf{e}_1 \\) (where \\( \\mathbf{e}_1 \\) is a basis vector), do not have a convergent Laplace integral. Similarly, multivector-valued functions with essential singularities or discontinuities may not be Laplace-transformable. Thus, the class of admissible functions is similar to the classical case: functions of exponential order with piecewise continuous grade components.\n\nTo illustrate the practical benefits of our approach, consider the analysis of an RLC circuit where voltages and currents are represented as multivectors to encode both magnitude and orientation (e.g., in three-phase systems). Using our GA Laplace transform, the circuit equations can be transformed into the Laplace domain while preserving the geometric relationships between quantities. This enables unified analysis of scalar and vector components, simplifies the handling of coupled equations, and provides a natural framework for incorporating geometric constraints, such as orthogonality or conservation laws.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews relevant background and related work; Section 3 introduces the formal definition and main properties of the GA Laplace transform; Section 4 discusses applications and limitations, including the RLC circuit example; and Section 5 concludes with directions for future research.\n\nReferences:\n[1] Doran, C., & Lasenby, A. (2003). Geometric Algebra for Physicists. Cambridge University Press.\n[2] Hitzer, E. (2013). Multivector Differential Calculus. Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, 23(1), 181\u2013195.\n[3] Ell, T. A., & Sangwine, S. J. (2007). Quaternion Fourier and Laplace Transforms for Signal and Image Processing. Advances in Imaging and Electron Physics, 140, 1\u201356.\n[4] Brackx, F., Delanghe, R., & Sommen, F. (1982). Clifford Analysis. Research Notes in Mathematics, 76."}}